            <ArticleTitle>Rehydration using solutions with and without glucose before emergency abdominal surgery in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>35-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The metabolic effects of six different solutions used for rehydration were studied in 88 children before emergency abdominal surgery. A volume corresponding to 5% of the body weight was given i.v. during 4 h (12.5 ml . kg-1 . h-1) between admission and surgery. The different solutions were: low glucose electrolyte solution (LGE), Rehydrex 2.5%, Rehydrex 5% containing 0.9, 2.5 and 5.0% glucose, respectively, Ringer, Ringer lactate and Ringerdex (30 mmol acetate . l-1). Venous blood samples were drawn before and after rehydration and analysed for glucose, insulin, alanine, 3-hydroxybutyrate (3-HB), non-esterified fatty acids (NEFA), glycerol and base excess. Before rehydration the plasma concentrations of NEFA, glycerol and 3-HB were elevated, while that of alanine was decreased. Plasma levels of NEFA and 3-HB remained unchanged, whereas glucose and insulin decreased after rehydration with glucose-free solutions, i.e. Ringer, Ringer lactate and Ringerdex. With solutions containing glucose the concentrations of NEFA and 3-HB decreased significantly. With Rehydrex 5% hyperglycaemia and hyperinsulinaemia were observed. Rehydration using LGE and especially Rehydrex 2.5% resulted in decreased lipid mobilization and ketonaemia without concomitant marked increase of glucose and insulin. LGE and Rehydrex 2.5% thus appeared to be the most appropriate solutions in a situation where moderate dehydration had to be corrected during a short period of time before emergency abdominal surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
